    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "Find the intersection points of:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p2-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$$\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2&=9\\\\y&=2\\end{aligned}$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(\\sqrt5,2)$ and $(-\\sqrt5,2)$.",
          "worked": "Substitute $y=2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+2^2&=9\\\\x^2&=5\\\\x&=\\pm\\sqrt5\\end{align*}$$\nThe intersection points are $(\\sqrt5,2)$ and $(-\\sqrt5,2)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p2-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$$\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2&=4\\\\x&=1\\end{aligned}$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(1,\\sqrt3)$ and $(1,-\\sqrt3)$.",
          "worked": "Substitute $x=1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}1^2+y^2&=4\\\\y^2&=3\\\\y&=\\pm\\sqrt3\\end{align*}$$\nThe intersection points are $(1,\\sqrt3)$ and $(1,-\\sqrt3)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p2-q6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$$\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2&=4\\\\y&=x+2\\end{aligned}$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(0,2)$ and $(-2,0)$.",
          "worked": "Substitute $y=x+2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+(x+2)^2&=4\\\\2x^2+4x+4&=4\\\\2x^2+4x&=0\\\\2x(x+2)&=0\\\\x&=0\\text{ or }-2\\end{align*}$$\nFor $x=0$, $y=0+2=2$. For $x=-2$, $y=-2+2=0$.\n\nThe intersection points are $(0,2)$ and $(-2,0)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p2-q6-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$$\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2&=16\\\\y&=4-\\sqrt{2}x\\end{aligned}$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(0,4)$ and $\\left(\\frac{8\\sqrt2}{3},-\\frac43\\right)$.\n\nSubstitute the line equation into the circle equation and factorise.",
          "worked": "Substitute $y=4-\\sqrt2x$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+(4-\\sqrt2x)^2&=16\\\\3x^2-8\\sqrt2x+16&=16\\\\3x^2-8\\sqrt2x&=0\\\\x(3x-8\\sqrt2)&=0\\\\x&=0\\text{ or }\\frac{8\\sqrt2}{3}\\end{align*}$$\nFor $x=0$, $y=4$.\n\nFor $x=\\frac{8\\sqrt2}{3}$:\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=4-\\sqrt2\\left(\\frac{8\\sqrt2}{3}\\right)\\\\y&=4-\\frac{16}{3}\\\\y&=-\\frac43\\end{align*}$$\nThe intersection points are $(0,4)$ and $\\left(\\frac{8\\sqrt2}{3},-\\frac43\\right)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      }
    ]
